1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:03,790
On Wednesday, John Healey delivers
his first Budget as Chancellor.

2
00:00:03,790 --> 00:00:07,840
The biggest tax change for landlords
this year won't be in his speech.

3
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:09,980
It was announced last year, November.

4
00:00:09,980 --> 00:00:15,360
It's already law and it starts in April
2027, whatever he says on the day.

5
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:18,300
In this video, I'll go through
what's already confirmed for

6
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:22,240
landlords, what's been ruled out,
and what's still only a rumour.

7
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:24,880
I'll show you what the
April 2027 change does

8
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:27,660
to a typical landlord's tax
bill, with real numbers.

9
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:29,410
And at the end, I'll go through four

10
00:00:29,410 --> 00:00:32,960
things worth checking in your
own portfolio before Wednesday.

11
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:35,580
Every Budget, the same thing happens.

12
00:00:35,640 --> 00:00:38,450
The headlines fill up with
what might change, and some

13
00:00:38,450 --> 00:00:41,440
landlords make big decisions
on the back of them.

14
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:45,480
If you already own a property that
isn't performing, that pressure is

15
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:49,280
worse, because you're already wondering
whether to hold it or sell it.

16
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:51,310
But a decision made on a rumour is

17
00:00:51,310 --> 00:00:53,670
made before you know
what's actually changing.

18
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:56,160
I'm Jake, the founder of Calibre Acquire.

19
00:00:56,160 --> 00:00:59,280
I've been in property for 14
years, and last year we transacted

20
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:04,640
on over £30 million in mixed-use buildings,
residential blocks and commercial property.

21
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:06,920
I help investors get out of
underperforming property

22
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:09,540
assets and into ones that
work harder for them.

23
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:11,880
Let's start with what's already confirmed,

24
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:14,240
because none of this depends on Wednesday.

25
00:01:14,280 --> 00:01:16,030
First, property income tax.

26
00:01:16,030 --> 00:01:21,070
From 6th of April 2027, rental
profit gets its own tax rates.

27
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:25,770
The basic rate goes from 20%
to 22%, the higher rate from

28
00:01:25,770 --> 00:01:31,990
40% to 42% and the additional
rate from 45% to 47%.

29
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:34,900
This was announced at last
November's Budget, and

30
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:37,160
it became law in March this year.

31
00:01:37,160 --> 00:01:42,620
The tax credit you get on mortgage
interest also goes up from 20% to 22%.

32
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:46,460
So landlords with bigger mortgages
feel slightly less of the increase.

33
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:50,280
There's also a change to how
your personal allowance is used.

34
00:01:50,280 --> 00:01:53,290
From April 2027, it goes
against your salary

35
00:01:53,290 --> 00:01:56,240
first, and only then
against your rental income.

36
00:01:56,240 --> 00:01:58,740
If you earn a good salary,
this probably makes no

37
00:01:58,740 --> 00:02:02,040
difference to you, because your job
already uses up your allowance.

38
00:02:02,040 --> 00:02:03,970
Then there's Making Tax Digital.

39
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,570
Since April this year, landlords with combined
rental, self-employed income over £50,000

40
00:02:09,570 --> 00:02:14,240
have had to keep digital records
and report to HMRC every quarter.

41
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:18,680
From April 2027, that
threshold drops to £30,000.

42
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:22,300
It's based on your income before
expenses, not your profit.

43
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:24,110
And then there's the
High Value Council Tax

44
00:02:24,110 --> 00:02:27,070
Surcharge, which most people
call the mansion tax.

45
00:02:27,070 --> 00:02:29,570
It applies to residential
property in England worth

46
00:02:29,570 --> 00:02:32,480
£2 million or more, from April 2028.

47
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:35,140
For most landlords watching
this, it won't apply.

48
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:38,200
If you're considering a property and
want to stress test it within a few

49
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:42,280
minutes, I built a set of free deal
calculators that let you do that.

50
00:02:42,280 --> 00:02:45,950
There's one for mixed-use, one for
HMOs and one for single residential.

51
00:02:46,130 --> 00:02:49,790
Comment SEND below and I'll send them
over, or use the link in the description.

52
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:54,200
Here's what the April 2027
change actually means in pounds.

53
00:02:54,220 --> 00:02:58,120
Take a landlord on a good salary, so
they're already a higher-rate taxpayer.

54
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:00,860
Their rental income,
after running costs but

55
00:03:00,860 --> 00:03:03,840
before mortgage interest,
is £30,000 a year.

56
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:06,520
Their mortgage interest is £12,000 a year.

57
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:11,480
So their real profit, the money they
actually keep before tax, is £18,000.

58
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:16,360
Today, they pay 40% on the full
£30,000, which is £12,000.

59
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:22,120
They get a 20% credit on the £12,000
of interest, which is £2,400 back.

60
00:03:22,120 --> 00:03:24,850
So their tax bill is £9,600.

61
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:31,760
From April 2027, it's 42% on
£30,000, which is £12,600.

62
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:37,840
The credit becomes 22% of
£12,000, which is £2,640 back.

63
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:41,460
So their tax bill is £9,960.

64
00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:43,840
That's £360 a year more.

65
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:46,480
On its own, that's not the
number to worry about.

66
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:52,720
The number to look at is £9,960
of tax on £18,000 of real profit.

67
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:55,450
That's an effective tax rate of 55%.

68
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:58,440
That's down to Section 24,
which stopped individual

69
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:01,440
landlords deducting their
mortgage interest in full.

70
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:06,720
The April 2027 change adds a bit more on
top of a cost that was already there.

71
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:07,680
I learned this myself.

72
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:10,440
I owned a one-bed flat in
Brentwood in my own name.

73
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:13,000
On paper, it yielded about 6%.

74
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:15,060
Once the service charge,
ground rent and tax

75
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:17,040
were paid, it was losing
money every month.

76
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:19,720
I sold it at a £30,000 loss to get it out.

77
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:21,640
Holding it for longer
would have cost me more.

78
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:24,350
Some landlords look at a
limited company as the answer.

79
00:04:24,350 --> 00:04:28,400
A company can deduct its
mortgage interest in full,

80
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:31,040
and it pays corporation
tax instead of income tax.

81
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:35,260
But taking money out of a company has
its own tax, and moving properties

82
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:39,460
you already own into a company can
trigger stamp duty and capital gains tax.

83
00:04:39,460 --> 00:04:42,180
It works well for some
people and badly for others,

84
00:04:42,180 --> 00:04:45,480
so speak to an accountant
before you make that move.

85
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:48,560
I'll cover it properly in a
separate video next month.

86
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:50,340
Now, what's been ruled out?

87
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:51,400
Stamp duty.

88
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:54,060
In July, the Prime
Minister said there would

89
00:04:54,060 --> 00:04:56,600
be no change to stamp duty in this Budget.

90
00:04:56,600 --> 00:04:59,700
He also ruled out replacing
stamp duty and council

91
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:02,720
tax with a single annual
property tax, for now.

92
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:05,020
So if you're buying,
your stamp duty bill on

93
00:05:05,020 --> 00:05:07,400
Thursday will be the same as it is today.

94
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:09,130
Then there's capital gains tax.

95
00:05:09,130 --> 00:05:12,570
There was talk of bringing it in line
with income tax, which would have

96
00:05:12,570 --> 00:05:17,280
taken the higher rate on property
gains from 24% to 40% or more.

97
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:20,480
The Chancellor is reported to
have rejected that, but it's

98
00:05:20,480 --> 00:05:24,730
been reported, not announced, and a
smaller increase is still possible.

99
00:05:24,730 --> 00:05:28,570
To put that in pounds, a higher-rate
landlord with a £200,000

100
00:05:28,570 --> 00:05:34,160
gain pays about £47,000 in tax
today, after the £3,000 allowance.

101
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:38,640
At 40%, that same gain would
have cost nearly £79,000.

102
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:40,400
That leaves what's still a rumour.

103
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:42,770
A smaller rise in capital gains tax.

104
00:05:42,870 --> 00:05:45,690
Some form of council tax
reform, which the Prime

105
00:05:45,690 --> 00:05:49,000
Minister has said he supports,
without giving any detail.

106
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,840
And wider tax rises, which the
Chancellor hasn't ruled out.

107
00:05:52,840 --> 00:05:55,860
I'm recording this before the
Budget, so anything I've called

108
00:05:55,860 --> 00:05:58,360
a rumour could be confirmed
or dropped on Wednesday.

109
00:05:58,360 --> 00:06:00,740
The Budget also isn't
the only thing moving.

110
00:06:00,740 --> 00:06:03,060
Bank of England rate is 3.75%.

111
00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:06,660
It was held in September, but three of the

112
00:06:06,660 --> 00:06:10,400
nine committee members
voted to raise it to 4%.

113
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:16,110
Inflation rose to 3.5% in
September, up from 3.1% in August.

114
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:19,220
The next rate decision is
on Thursday 5th November.

115
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:23,140
So here are the four things
worth checking before Wednesday.

116
00:06:23,140 --> 00:06:27,180
First, run each property
on the April 2027 rates

117
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:29,840
and work out what you
actually keep after tax.

118
00:06:29,840 --> 00:06:33,240
Not the yield on paper, the
money that ends up with you.

119
00:06:33,240 --> 00:06:36,480
Second, if you're selling and
the capital gains rumours worry

120
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:39,760
you, it's worth knowing that
for tax purposes, a sale

121
00:06:39,760 --> 00:06:44,200
usually happens when contracts are
exchanged, not when it completes.

122
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:46,530
The last time capital gains went up, in

123
00:06:46,530 --> 00:06:50,160
October 2024, the new rates
applied from Budget day.

124
00:06:50,160 --> 00:06:53,220
But you can't get a sale
to exchange in two days.

125
00:06:53,240 --> 00:06:55,420
One of our sales, a block of five flats on

126
00:06:55,420 --> 00:06:58,960
Holloway Road, took 12 months from
agreeing the deal to completion.

127
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:02,220
Rushing a sale because of a
rumour usually means accepting

128
00:07:02,220 --> 00:07:05,760
a weaker price or skipping
checks on the legal work.

129
00:07:05,760 --> 00:07:10,240
Third, check whether Making Tax
Digital applies to you from April.

130
00:07:10,240 --> 00:07:14,440
If your rental and self-employed
income before expenses was over

131
00:07:14,440 --> 00:07:19,140
£30,000 in 2025 to 2026 tax year, it does.

132
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:21,840
Fourth, separate the
property from the headlines.

133
00:07:21,840 --> 00:07:26,160
If a property is performing well, a
Budget rumour isn't a reason to sell it.

134
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:28,180
If it isn't performing, that's a reason to

135
00:07:28,180 --> 00:07:31,240
look at it, whatever the
Chancellor says on Wednesday.

136
00:07:31,240 --> 00:07:34,580
The decision should come from the
property, not from the speech.

137
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:36,960
If you found this useful,
send it to someone

138
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:39,560
who's looking at their own
portfolio before the Budget.

139
00:07:39,560 --> 00:07:42,870
It could save them significant
amount on the next deal.

140
